===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Unconventional, especially in [[habit]] or [[dress]].
#* '''2001''', {{w|Baz Luhrmann}}, {{w|Craig Pearce}} (scriptwriters), ''{{w|Moulin Rouge!}}'',
#*: Please tell me you're not one of Toulouse's oh-so-talented, charmingly '''bohemian''', tragically impoverished protégés!
#* '''2002''', Michael Hicks, ''Henry Cowell, '''Bohemian''''', {{w|University of Illinois Press}}, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=U_AAiFRmTM0C&pg=PA4&dq=%22more%7Cmost+bohemian%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjD7-7sppKBAxUa0GEKHa1VCucQ6AF6BAhcEAI#v=onepage&q=%22more%7Cmost%20bohemian%22&f=false page 4],
#*: Cowell's earlier, more '''bohemian''' years are not only the most interesting, they comprise the only part of his life for which one could reasonably attempt to do the kind of work that I have tried to do here.
#* '''2014''', Shabana Mir, ''Muslim American Women on Campus'', {{w|University of North Carolina Press}}, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=7-2VAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA18&dq=%22more%7Cmost+bohemian%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjD7-7sppKBAxUa0GEKHa1VCucQ6AF6BAg8EAI#v=onepage&q=%22more%7Cmost%20bohemian%22&f=false page 18],
#*: Georgetown, a Jesuit university and a "hot big city school" (Mathews 2007:53): is popularly believed to be "hotter," wealthier, more '''bohemian''', more liberal, and more laid back than its secular counterpart, GWU.
#* '''2017''', Shawn Chandler Bingham, '''''Bohemian''' Groves, Grooves, Gardens, and Guns'', Shawn Chandler Bingham, Lindsey A. Freeman (editors), ''The '''Bohemian''' South'', {{w|University of North Carolina Press}}, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=6IDPDgAAQBAJ&pg=PA271&dq=%22more%7Cmost+bohemian%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjD7-7sppKBAxUa0GEKHa1VCucQ6AF6BAhmEAI#v=onepage&q=%22more%7Cmost%20bohemian%22&f=false page 271],
#*: The magazine's aesthetic also bends it into more '''bohemian''' territory. Food articles often have botanical illustrations of lesser-eaten greenery like endive and watercress, drawn by artist John Burgoyne.

